          <zone forceUpdate='true' h='13701' id='18' type-v2='text' w='99034' x='483' y='85475'>
            <formatted-text>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='11'>Key Insights</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='11'>• Abnormal patients represent roughly two-thirds of the dataset.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='11'>• Pelvic incidence tends to be higher in abnormal spinal conditions.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='11'>• Pelvic incidence and sacral slope show a strong positive relationship.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='11'>• Vertebral displacement (spondylolisthesis) is significantly larger in abnormal patients and appears to be the strongest indicator of spinal disorder.</run>
            </formatted-text>
